    "question_text": "During a rescue search, a helicopter flew b kilometres\nwest from point X to point Y, then changed course\nand flew 10.7 kilometres north to point Z. If point Z\nis on a bearing of 335° T from point X:\nNow, if the distance that the helicopter must fly\nbetween point Z and point X is d kilometres,\ncalculate d to one decimal place.",
    "problem_index": 327,
    "model": "mistralai/mistral-small-3.2-24b-instruct",
    "run_number": 1,
    "model_output": {
      "ANSWER": "14.9 km",
      "REASONING": "To find the distance d between points Z and X, we can use the Pythagorean theorem. The helicopter flies b km west and then 10.7 km north, forming a right triangle with the hypotenuse being the distance d. The angle given is 335°, which is equivalent to -25° (335° - 360°). The cosine of -25° is the same as the cosine of 25°. Therefore, b = d * cos(25°). We need to find d, so we rearrange the equation: d = 10.7 / sin(25°). Using a calculator, sin(25°) ≈ 0.4226. Thus, d = 10.7 / 0.4226 ≈ 14.9 km."
    },
    "status": "OK"
  },
  {
    "case_id": 1,
    "case_label": "full_text + full_diagram",
