    "image_path": "C:\\Users\\arpit.chabbra\\Downloads\\noise_injection\\DATASET\\Dataset_split\\diagrams\\Setting_1&2\\FTFD\\364.jpg",
    "question_text": "On a suspension bridge, the top of a concrete tower is 35.5\nmetres above the bridge and the connection point for the\nmain cable is 65.9 metres from the tower. Assuming\nthe tower and bridge are perpendicular, what is the length\nof the cable h to two decimal places?",
    "problem_index": 364,
    "model": "mistralai/mistral-small-3.2-24b-instruct",
    "run_number": 1,
    "model_output": {
      "ANSWER": "66.22",
      "REASONING": "To find the length of the cable h, we can use the Pythagorean theorem. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right-angled triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. In this case, the height of the tower (35.5 meters) and the horizontal distance from the tower to the cable connection point (65.9 meters) form the two legs of the right-angled triangle, and the cable length h is the hypotenuse. Using the Pythagorean theorem: h^2 = 35.5^2 + 65.9^2. First, calculate the squares of the two legs: 35.5^2 = 1260.25 and 65.9^2 = 4343.21. Next, add these squares together: 1260.25 + 4343.21 = 5603.46. Finally, take the square root of this sum to find h: h = sqrt(5603.46) = 66.22 meters (rounded to two decimal places)."
    },
    "status": "OK"
  },
